---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site.js';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const href = (url) => `${base}${url.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Policy | Plain Act"
  description="How Plain Act develops, edits, sources, reviews, updates, and corrects practical management content."
  path="/editorial-policy/"
>
  <section class="hero policy-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[{name:'Home', url:'/'}, {name:'Editorial Policy', url:'/editorial-policy/'}]} />
      <p class="eyebrow">Editorial policy</p>
      <h1>Useful first. Clear about how it was made.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Plain Act publishes practical management guidance for first-time and working managers. This policy explains the standards used for scope, originality, source handling, production tools, editing, updates, corrections, and commercial independence.</p>
      <p class="small">Policy reviewed: August 31, 2026.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-sm">
    <div class="container policy-layout">
      <nav class="policy-nav" aria-label="Editorial policy sections">
        <a href="#mission">Mission</a><a href="#scope">Scope</a><a href="#originality">Originality</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a><a href="#production">Production tools</a><a href="#review">Review</a><a href="#updates">Updates</a><a href="#commercial">Commercial independence</a><a href="#errors">Report an error</a>
      </nav>

      <div class="policy-content">
        <section id="mission"><p class="eyebrow">01 — Mission</p><h2>Make management guidance usable under pressure.</h2><p>Plain Act is built around a narrow editorial purpose: help managers see a workplace problem clearly enough to take a responsible next action without unnecessary noise, hype, or performance.</p><p>A page should still be useful if all advertising, affiliate links, and purchase buttons are ignored.</p></section>

        <section id="scope"><p class="eyebrow">02 — Scope</p><h2>What Plain Act covers.</h2><p>Primary topics include first-time management, authority, delegation, feedback, difficult conversations, meetings, workload and capacity, managing up, former-peer transitions, decision timing, and the first 30–90 days in a management role.</p><p>The publication provides general management guidance. It is not positioned as legal, employment-law, clinical, medical, financial, or employer-specific HR advice. A reader may need qualified professional or internal-policy guidance when a situation carries those risks.</p></section>

        <section id="originality"><p class="eyebrow">03 — Originality and utility</p><h2>Pages need an independent reason to exist.</h2><p>Indexable articles, guides, and tools are expected to solve a distinct reader problem and add practical value through examples, scripts, checklists, decision rules, scenarios, templates, agendas, or worked applications where appropriate.</p><p>Plain Act does not treat copied definitions, lightly rewritten third-party material, synonym replacement, mass page generation, or generic word-count padding as acceptable editorial value. A weak page may be merged, noindexed, rebuilt, or removed instead of expanded with filler.</p></section>

        <section id="sources"><p class="eyebrow">04 — Sources and factual claims</p><h2>External facts should be distinguishable from editorial judgment.</h2><p>When a page materially relies on a research finding, law, platform rule, product specification, price, policy, or other externally verifiable fact, the source should be identified or linked when practical and appropriate.</p><p>Illustrative workplace examples, scripts, and management judgments are not presented as research findings unless a source is explicitly provided. A source supports the factual claim for which it is cited; it does not automatically validate every recommendation on the page.</p></section>

        <section id="production"><p class="eyebrow">05 — Production tools</p><h2>Software assistance is not treated as authority.</h2><p>Plain Act may use software tools, including AI-assisted tools, during research organization, drafting, editing, formatting, coding, or production. These tools are not named as experts, reviewers, or evidence sources merely because they produced text or code.</p><p>Publication responsibility remains with {site.publisher}. AI-generated or software-generated output is not, by itself, treated as proof of a factual claim. Where factual accuracy matters, the relevant source or live product behavior should be checked independently.</p></section>

        <section id="review"><p class="eyebrow">06 — Editing and review</p><h2>Review is tied to the page, not a fictional reviewer identity.</h2><p>Editorial review can include checking the page's user intent, examples, internal links, overlapping topics, claims, structure, metadata, and whether the advice remains useful without a purchase. Technical pages may also be checked against the current production site or source configuration.</p><p>Plain Act does not create fictional experts, named reviewers, credentials, testimonials, or years of experience to make a page appear more authoritative.</p></section>

        <section id="updates"><p class="eyebrow">07 — Updates</p><h2>Dates should describe real events.</h2><p>Material may be revised for factual accuracy, clarity, usefulness, structure, broken links, changed third-party information, stronger examples, or a better distinction from another page.</p><p>An “updated”, “reviewed”, or similar date should be shown only when an actual review or material update occurred. Minor styling or automated deployment changes do not automatically justify presenting an editorial review date.</p></section>

        <section id="commercial"><p class="eyebrow">08 — Commercial independence</p><h2>Commercial links do not decide editorial conclusions.</h2><p>Plain Act publishes paid books and may use clearly disclosed affiliate relationships. Those commercial relationships do not change the standard that free editorial pages should provide a complete core answer without requiring a purchase.</p><p>When a recommendation or outbound link can generate a commission, the relationship should be disclosed in a way that is reasonably visible to the reader. See the <a href={href('/affiliate-disclosure/')}>Affiliate Disclosure</a>.</p></section>

        <section id="errors" class="notice"><p class="eyebrow">09 — Corrections</p><h2>Report an error.</h2><p>If you find a factual error, broken source, misleading statement, outdated detail, or material omission, email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. Include the page URL, the exact point that needs review, and supporting evidence when available.</p><p>Reports are handled under the <a href={href('/corrections-policy/')}>Corrections Policy</a>. Plain Act does not promise real-time review, but confirmed material issues may be corrected, clarified, expanded, or removed.</p></section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .policy-hero h1{max-width:14ch}.policy-layout{display:grid;grid-template-columns:220px minmax(0,760px);gap:3rem;align-items:start;justify-content:center}.policy-nav{position:sticky;top:110px;display:grid;gap:.15rem;border-left:1px solid var(--border);padding-left:1rem}.policy-nav a{text-decoration:none;color:#aaa;padding:.35rem 0;font-size:.9rem}.policy-nav a:hover{color:#fff}.policy-content{display:grid;gap:3.4rem}.policy-content section{scroll-margin-top:120px}.policy-content h2{max-width:18ch}.policy-content p{max-width:70ch}.policy-content .notice{padding:1.4rem;border-left:4px solid var(--accent);background:#1a1d20}
  @media(max-width:800px){.policy-layout{grid-template-columns:1fr}.policy-nav{position:static;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));border-left:0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:0 0 1rem}}
</style>
